= Microarchitecture =

== Overview ==

{{:Microarchitecture/Simple}}

A '''microarchitecture''' is an implementation organization for an instruction set architecture, including datapaths, control logic, pipelines, issue and retirement rules, cache hierarchies, prediction mechanisms and memory interfaces. An ISA defines programmer-visible behavior, but ordinarily leaves these internal choices open. The difference permits independent implementations of a common software contract.

== Design boundaries ==

A pipeline may improve throughput by overlapping instruction stages without reducing the latency of every individual instruction. Superscalar designs may issue several operations in a cycle when dependencies and resources permit. Out-of-order designs maintain dependency information and reorder some execution, then recover from mispredictions or exceptions. Branch prediction and speculation can reduce stalls but introduce recovery costs and security concerns.

A cache is an implementation feature that affects performance and timing. Its exact configuration is not necessarily specified by the ISA, and its existence does not imply a single memory-consistency model. ISA-visible ordering guarantees and implementation-level buffering must be distinguished.<ref name="riscv_core">RISC-V International, [https://docs.riscv.org/reference/isa/unpriv/unpriv-index.html ''Unprivileged ISA Manual''], especially RVWMO and memory-access rules.</ref><ref name="boom_core">Berkeley Architecture Research, [https://docs.boom-core.org/en/latest/ ''BOOM Documentation''], pipeline and out-of-order scheduling implementation.</ref>

== Evaluation ==

Performance depends on workload, compiler, frequency, instruction mix, memory behavior and power/thermal constraints. A simplified identity is execution time = retired instruction count × average cycles per retired instruction / clock frequency. This identity is a useful accounting device when the definitions and measurement period match; it is not a predictive physical law for variable-frequency heterogeneous execution.

Correctness is evaluated against the appropriate ISA/platform contract, including faults, interrupts, memory ordering and supported extensions. Microarchitectural security also requires testing information leakage through timing and shared resources, even when architectural registers appear correct.<ref name="spectre_core">Kocher et al., [https://spectreattack.com/spectre.pdf ''Spectre Attacks: Exploiting Speculative Execution''] (2018).</ref>

== Connections ==

Microarchitecture is a narrower implementation topic than [[Related to::Computer architecture|computer architecture]] and is related to [[Related to::Compiler|compilation]], [[Related to::Operating system|operating systems]] and [[Related to::Computational complexity|resource analysis]]. The standalone Instruction set architecture article is drafted in an open review PR rather than deployed or merged, so it is not a hard dependency of this page.

